              "command": "/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('docs/story/concepts.md');s=p.read_text().replace('Selection: pending user choice. No full storyboard or final appearance committed.', '''Selection: **Concept 1 — The bring-up fixture**, chosen 13 October 2026 under the user's instruction to choose the recommended concept.\n\nWhy: Ada personally built the fixture. A bench checklist lets a firmware hiring manager inspect the relationship between firmware responsibility, board access, physical observation and evidence limits. The symbolic enclosure and probe are less directly tied to something Ada made. The fixture remains explanatory geometry rather than a reconstruction of real hardware.\n\nStoryboard is now in storyboard.md for approval. Final appearance and wording remain later choices.''').replace('# Concept studies — awaiting selection','# Concept studies — fixture selected');p.write_text(s)\np=Path('docs/story/facts.md');s=p.read_text().replace('# Facts — stop 1, awaiting Ada\\\\'s answers','# Facts — source reading and confirmed corrections').replace('No user answers received yet. Unanswered claims remain excluded from future page copy.','The initial questions above are a historical record. User-confirmed corrections below supersede them; remaining unknowns are omissions, not blockers.');s+='''\n\n## Stable references for storyboard\n\nAll user-confirmed references below refer to the corrections above and the user's messages. Source-only F10 is separately identified.\n\n- F1: public Ada Mensah/Mosslink; firmware-role target; audience is firmware hiring manager.\n- F2: Ada wrote firmware, scheduled power states and built bring-up fixture.\n- F3: colleague designed PCB and analogue front end; Ada does not claim hardware-design ownership.\n- F4: state table, timing diagram, current trace and bench checklists; bring-up checks a new board's basic operation.\n- F5: permitted local teaching drawings only; Sleep/Measure/Transmit illustrative, no actual states/durations/current values, original wiring or measured evidence; no fixture photo/trace available.\n- F6: radio awake after failed transmission observed in current trace; no cause/fix/timing/later outcome confirmed.\n- F7: two-year projection from bench currents/assumed cycle; longest run 72 hours; no supporting field endurance/weather testing.\n- F8: forty engineering samples assembled, six bench tested, none to customers, no field pilot/launch.\n- F9: one preliminary emissions check passed; full qualification unfinished, no certification/qualification claim.\n- F10: C, oscilloscopes and logic analysers listed in material/cv.md; user authorizes corrected CV from confirmed facts and omission of unknown facts. Tools were supplied as source facts with no contradiction; include as listed skills, without proficiency ranking or years.\n- F11: lesson: a firmware decision needs a physical observation; estimates and observed runs support different claims.\n- F12: public example email link, printable downloadable HTML CV, Mosslink-only project page, English, ordinary navigation, share preview/icon/useful missing page.\n- F13: no employer/dates/current employment assertion, testimonials/blog/chat/translation/form, external purchase/download/skill installation or publishing; legal/hosting details deferred.\n''';p.write_text(s)\nPY\ncat > docs/story/storyboard.md <<'EOF'\n# Storyboard — The bring-up fixture\n\nStatus: proposed 13 October 2026; awaiting user's approval. Concept 1 selected by the user authorizing the recommendation. Appearance and final wording remain later choices. No new skill or remote asset is needed.\n\n## Question, form and answer\n\nOpening question: **How do you connect firmware to a physical observation?**\n\nPremises: Ada wrote Mosslink firmware, scheduled power states and built its bring-up fixture (F2). Ada wants a firmware hiring manager to understand the connection between code and a measurable signal (F1/F11).\n\nForm: a bench checklist. Purpose → Setup → Exercise → Observe → Record limits → Handover. This is a narrative order, not a claim about Mosslink's historical test chronology or a reproduction of Ada's actual checklist.\n\nObject: one explanatory bring-up fixture made of six substantial contact modules, with bodies, metallic pins and labelled inset faces. The same six modules persist throughout and reunite at the close. They stand for conceptual relationships; six does not stand for six tested samples, actual contact count or recovered hardware construction. No paper, sheets or card collection.\n\nClosing meaning: connect a firmware decision to a physical observation through bench access, then limit the claim to the evidence. Ada's firmware/fixture work establishes the personal contribution. The radio-awake note establishes an observation without a confirmed explanation. The projection and 72-hour run establish that different evidence supports different claims (F2/F6/F7/F11). The close does not imply successful diagnosis, a fix, or completed testing.\n\n## Chapters\n\n| Chapter | Name in the form | Role | What it tells | What the pieces do | What you can touch |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Purpose | Opening / who | Ada, Mosslink, firmware and fixture work; central question | Form the complete contact fixture from the start; gently settle with scroll | Email, CV view/download, Mosslink page, skip to Handover |\n| 2 | Setup | Who / contribution | Ada's firmware, power scheduling and fixture; colleague's PCB/analogue responsibility | Open into two groups, distinguishing responsibilities; inset labels remain on solid modules | Link to Mosslink contribution detail |\n| 3 | Exercise | How | Working documents, meaning of bring-up, permitted teaching cycle | Arrange paired modules along a conceptual command/access/observation path, then a three-pair loop | Open either local explanatory SVG; project link |\n| 4 | Observe | What | Radio-awake behavior seen after failed transmission; no confirmed cause or resolution | Separate observation from the modules representing explanation/claim; no animated waveform | Mosslink observation detail |\n| 5 | Record limits | Evidence / outcomes | Battery projection vs run; samples vs use; preliminary check vs qualification | Split into matched groups for evidence categories, with bounded labels; no quantitative scaling | Project evidence table; CV |\n| 6 | Handover | Close | Answer to opening question and next action for a hiring manager | Reunite as a whole fixture, keeping the code/access/observation/claim relationships visible | Email, HTML CV view/download, Mosslink page, return to Purpose |\n\nNo employment-history chapter, additional project or testimonial section: the confirmed material does not support them.\n\n## Beats — no final captions yet\n\nProposed exception: **10 meaningful beats, totalling 12 screen heights**. The engine's usual full-story range is 12–22 beats, but this material has one project and limited publishable evidence. Ten avoids repeating claims or adding unsupported chronology. Approval of this storyboard includes this shorter structure.\n\nEvery face label below is explanatory navigation, not a claim of a real component or firmware state. Real figures appear in ordinary HTML with their qualifications; no count, spacing, pin glow, travel speed or shape measures real time/current/sample quantity.\n\n| Beat | Screens | How the pieces stand | What is on their faces | What the chapter adds | Working title / fact references for later caption |\n|---|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| purpose.fixture | 1.5 | Complete two-row fixture, already recognisable; modest settling during scroll | CODE / STATE / BOARD / ACCESS / SIGNAL / CLAIM | Nothing | Firmware at the bench — F1/F2/F4/F11; name, work and question |\n| setup.contribution | 1 | Three foreground modules with the other three still connected behind | FIRMWARE / SCHEDULING / FIXTURE; background relationship labels | Nothing | Ada's contribution — F2 |\n| setup.boundary | 1 | Two groups with a clear gap; all modules remain visible | ADA / FIRMWARE / FIXTURE; COLLEAGUE / PCB / ANALOGUE | Nothing | Responsibility boundary — F2/F3; PCB explained as printed circuit board |\n| exercise.documents | 1.25 | Three paired solid modules follow conceptual command → access → observation order | STATE TABLE / TIMING / COMMAND / ACCESS / TRACE / OBSERVE | Local bring-up SVG in a labelled HTML figure, available without scene | From command to bench — F4/F5; meaning of bring-up and working documents |\n| exercise.cycle | 1.25 | Three pairs form a loop, without a timer or moving pulse | SLEEP / TEACHING / MEASURE / TEACHING / TRANSMIT / TEACHING | Local power-states SVG in labelled HTML figure | Illustrative scheduling — F2/F5; teaching sequence only, no actual firmware/current/time |\n| observe.radio | 1.5 | One pair comes forward as observation; remaining modules separated as context/claim boundaries | RADIO / AWAKE; OBSERVATION / TRACE NOTE / CAUSE UNKNOWN / OUTCOME UNKNOWN | Nothing; no fabricated trace image or waveform | Radio-awake observation — F6; after failed transmission, no confirmed cause/fix/timing/resolution |\n| limits.battery | 1.25 | Two distinct groups represent projection and observed-run evidence; neither larger than the other | PROJECTED / BENCH CURRENT / ASSUMED CYCLE; OBSERVED RUN / 72 HOURS / LIMITS | Nothing | Battery evidence — F7; two-year projection, 72-hour run, no supporting field/weather test |\n| limits.samples | 1 | Fixture stays joined, with two groups highlighted by face labels rather than module count | ASSEMBLED / 40 SAMPLES / ENGINEERING; BENCH TESTED / 6 SAMPLES / NO CUSTOMERS | Nothing | Samples and use — F8; no pilot/launch, no individual total-testing claim |\n| limits.emissions | 1 | Small separation between preliminary check and programme status, without pass/fail spectacle | PRELIMINARY / EMISSIONS / CHECK PASSED; PROGRAMME / UNFINISHED / STATUS | Nothing | Qualification boundary — F9; no certified/qualified claim |\n| handover.whole | 1.25 | Whole fixture reunited at rest, with no ongoing idle motion at the conclusion | CODE / STATE / BOARD / ACCESS / SIGNAL / CLAIM | Ordinary HTML contact/download links | Claims follow evidence — F2/F6/F7/F11/F12; explicit answer plus actions |\n\nTotal: 1.5 + 1 + 1 + 1.25 + 1.25 + 1.5 + 1.25 + 1 + 1 + 1.25 = **12 screens**.\n\n## Someone with thirty seconds\n\nThe opening gives Ada's name, the firmware-role focus, Mosslink and her specific firmware/power-scheduling/fixture contribution. Explain firmware as code that controls a physical device, and bring-up as checking a new board's basic operation. This is supported by F1/F2/F4, without asserting a current job title.\n\nThe first screen includes a compact evidence line linking the two-year projection to its bench-current basis and identifying the longest run as 72 hours, with access to the full limitations (F7). This supplies concrete project evidence with its limits; do not suggest a measured trace or report is publicly available.\n\nEmail, CV view/download and Mosslink details are reachable immediately. A visible ordinary chapter navigation jumps to each of the six chapters; a skip link reaches Handover. Navigation remains available with the canvas disabled. Final opening wording is not decided here.\n\n## Without the scene\n\nAll factual claims, headings, evidence qualifications and links live in ordinary server-rendered HTML. The canvas is explanatory and takes no pointer events. No factual claim exists only on a 3D face.\n\n| Where | What the visitor gets |\n|---|---|\n| Phone | Same chapter/beat order, centred fixture above the caption, sized to available space; ordinary compact navigation and visible links. At very short heights, prioritise complete text and static explanation over squeezing an unreadable scene. No horizontal scrolling. |\n| Reduced motion | Cut between settled poses; no interpolation, sway, idle movement or animated caption entrances. All chapters and links remain available. |\n| No WebGL / failed scene | A compact HTML reading layout, figures for the two supplied explanations and a local static fixture illustration with an explicit conceptual-geometry label. No blank canvas gap. |\n| No JavaScript | Same full HTML story, navigation, email, project and HTML CV links; all figures and static explanation remain readable. CV download uses a normal same-origin anchor; printing uses browser Print. |\n\nThe static fixture illustration will be an original explanatory SVG derived from our geometry, with a useful accessible description. It is not a fixture photograph. Preserve both supplied SVG originals; retain their MIT/provenance details and record any adaptations.\n\n## Appearance studies after storyboard approval\n\nThe user will scroll the opening, observation and closing spine in these three looks and choose one. These are proposals, not a selected final look.\n\n| Look | Ground | Ink | Accent | Fixture material |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Warm bench | Warm off-white #f2f0e9 | Charcoal #202932 | Oxide #8d471e | Matte deep-green bodies, brass pins, cream inset labels |\n| Instrument dark | Deep blue-black #111c22 | Off-white #f3eee2 | Amber #efb475 | Graphite bodies, muted metal pins, light engraved labels |\n| Clean lab | Pale grey #e8eeed | Deep teal #163831 | Copper #a35d38 | Grey-green bodies, brushed-metal pins, dark inset labels |\n\nTwo local system-font stacks: system-ui for readable page text, ui-monospace for checklist labels and module faces. No external font/asset service. Use spacing and typographic weight for hierarchy; no extra font purchase/download.\n\n## Everything else the site needs\n\n| Item | Build, later or never | Who decided / scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| Way round the page | Build | User requests ordinary navigation; assistant proposes Home, Mosslink, CV and Email plus six chapter anchors/skip link |\n| Page for each project | Build | User; only /projects/mosslink |\n| Every project listed | Build | User; Mosslink is the only project, no padded grid |\n| Contact and CV | Build | User; mailto:ada@example.com, /cv for reading/printing, downloadable standalone /downloads/ada-mensah-cv.html |\n| What others say | Never | User; no testimonials or invented quotes |\n| Languages | Build English only | User; no translation |\n| Share preview and icon | Build | User; locally generated raster preview and SVG icon, page-specific titles/descriptions |\n| Missing-page page | Build | User; useful explanation plus Home, Mosslink and CV links |\n| Blog | Never | User |\n| Chat | Never | User |\n| Contact form | Never | User |\n| Legal notice / hosting jurisdiction | Later | User; wait for publication decision, no compliance claim |\n| Publishing / public URL | Later | User; keep local until explicit instruction |\n| External assets / helper skills | Never for this build | User; use available engine, local fonts/drawings and own geometry |\n\n## Supporting-page contents\n\n### /projects/mosslink\n\nOrdinary HTML case study, navigable independently of the scroll story. Sections:\n\n1. Project: Mosslink environmental sensor; Ada's firmware-role interest (F1, CV source for project description).\n2. Contribution: firmware, power scheduling, fixture; explicit colleague PCB/analogue division (F2/F3).\n3. Working method: state table, timing diagram, current trace and bench checklist; definition of bring-up (F4).\n4. Two local explanatory figures with accurate accessible descriptions, source/provenance and limits (F5). No unavailable photo/trace/diagram placeholders implying evidence exists.\n5. Radio-awake observation after failed transmission, with unknown cause/fix/timing/later outcome stated (F6).\n6. Evidence table: projection versus run, samples versus bench tests, preliminary emissions versus unfinished qualification (F7/F8/F9). Team quantities remain project-level statements.\n7. Lesson and links back to the corresponding main-story chapters, email and CV (F11/F12).\n\n### /cv and standalone downloadable HTML\n\nSame corrected content in both: Ada Mensah; seeking embedded firmware roles involving physical devices; public example email; Mosslink contribution and collaboration boundary; listed tools C, oscilloscopes, logic analysers and working documents; evidence/status with all qualifications preserved. No employer, dates, asserted current role, hardware ownership, launch/pilot/certification, photograph, extra project, award or testimonial.\n\nDownload is a complete self-contained HTML file with inline print CSS and system fonts, readable offline. Print layout keeps headings with related text, removes navigation and preserves evidence qualifications. /cv includes View/Download guidance and ordinary download link. No PDF or email delivery claim.\n\n### Share preview, icon and missing page\n\nShare preview: local 1200×630 PNG with Ada's name, embedded firmware role focus, Mosslink and a conceptual fixture illustration; no metrics without their qualifiers. Icon: simple original contact-pin/fixture mark. Both recorded as original explanatory assets. Set local metadata references and titles/descriptions; do not invent a public canonical URL. External crawler/public share behavior cannot be verified until publication; validate image/metadata locally.\n\nMissing page: clear missing-address message, normal links to Home, Mosslink and CV, preserving site identity. Do not send a visitor to an external page or expose framework diagnostics.\n\n## Decisions made for the user\n\n- Choose fixture under explicit instruction to use recommendation; record why in concepts.md.\n- Keep six tangible modules and a bench-checklist structure; no literal construction/count inference.\n- Propose 10 beats / 12 screen heights because one project does not justify padding; ask approval as part of this storyboard.\n- Use chapter order as a reading structure, not a project chronology.\n- Preserve meaning and links in HTML; supply static explanatory geometry for fallback.\n- Use system-ui and ui-monospace, three material-based appearance studies and a later look choice.\n- Use one project route and matching printable/viewable plus standalone downloadable HTML CV.\n- Use locally generated share/icon assets; defer public canonical URL, hosting and legal decisions.\n\n## Approval needed\n\nApprove this script, including the shorter 10-beat structure, or specify changes. Approval permits the opening/observation/ending spine in three looks. The user chooses a look after reviewing the runnable spine, then chooses final wording after the remaining chapters are built. Final site acceptance follows actual review and full browser/fallback checks.\nEOF\ncat >> docs/story/decided-for-you.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Storyboard decisions — 13 October 2026\n\n- Selected the bring-up fixture under the user's instruction to choose our recommendation. Reason: Ada built the fixture; code/access/observation/evidence limits are direct to her work. Change before storyboard approval by choosing another concept.\n- Proposed a 10-beat, 12-screen story across six checklist chapters. Reason: one project and limited available evidence do not justify 12–22 beats; shorter structure requires storyboard approval. Change screen heights/count in storyboard before implementation.\n- Proposed system-ui page type and ui-monospace labels, ordinary chapter/site links, one standalone project route and matched readable/downloadable printable HTML CV. Reason: local availability, access without scene and user's scope. Paths and type are adjustable without altering facts.\n- Proposed own static fixture SVG, local share-preview PNG and icon, with accurate explanatory labels/provenance. Reason: no actual photo or trace is available. Change assets only within user's local-asset authorization.\n- Appearance studies will be Warm bench, Instrument dark and Clean lab; none is selected. Final wording waits for its later user choice.\nEOF\npython3 - <<'PY'